plan.plan:
{
  "SECTION_1_TRAJECTORY_DESIGN": {
    "assumptions_and_kinematic_basis": "Arm is a generic PUMA-like 6-axis elbow configuration with ~1.8 m reach; axes 4/5/6 form the wrist with joint 5 (pitch) the wrist singularity axis. Wrist singularity occurs when theta5 -> 0 (axes 4 and 6 collinear), making the Jacobian rank-deficient. All quantities below are stated symbolically with numeric defaults for the stated geometry (900 mm weld, tool passes within 140 mm of singularity at midpoint).",
    "condition_number_calculation": "Condition number is computed each planner tick (10 ms) from the 6x6 Jacobian J(q) in the tool-control frame: kappa = sigma_max(J)/sigma_min(J), the singular values from J = U*diag(sigma_1..sigma_6)*V^T (SVD, or sqrt of eigenvalues of J*J^T, computable as a closed-form from the analytical Jacobian in ~50 us on the controller). Cost matrix W = diag(1,1,1, 0.2,0.2,0.2) weights translational vs rotational rows so kappa reflects weld-relevant dexterity: kappa = sigma_max(WJ)/sigma_min(WJ). Threshold policy: kappa_alarm = 30 (well above the healthy-path baseline kappa ~ 4-6, well below the dangerous regime kappa > 100 where commanded joint speeds blow up); kappa_replan = 20 is the hard ceiling the planner must stay under. The original path violates this: kappa rises from 4 to >400 because theta5 passes within 140 mm-equivalent (~14 deg) of zero at midpoint; sigma_min collapses as sin(theta5) -> 0.",
    "singularity_avoidance_redesign": "Redesign has two independent mechanisms. (1) Wrist reorientation (preferred): use the null-space of the Cartesian task (the weld line fixes 3 translational DOF and tool roll about the weld axis is free) to bias theta5 away from zero. The tool axis is kept on the weld tangent, but theta4 (wrist roll) is offset by a constant delta4 = +25 deg chosen so theta5 at the worst point moves from ~14 deg to ~39 deg: sin(39 deg) = 0.63, raising sigma_min by roughly the factor sin(39)/sin(14) = 2.6 and cutting kappa by the same factor. (2) Path offset fallback: if process engineering forbids wrist reorientation (torch angle constraint), offset the flange path laterally by 60 mm perpendicular to the weld plane while holding the tool tip on the exact weld line via a compensated tool transform; the wrist center then clears the singular cylinder by 200 mm. Both keep the weld seam geometry exact to within +-0.2 mm.",
    "velocity_profile": "Trapezoidal-with-jerk-limited profile (S-curve) on path coordinate s in [0, 900 mm]: acceleration phase 0-60 mm at 12 mm/s^2 mean (jerk-limited 120 mm/s^3), cruise 12 mm/s for 780 mm, symmetric deceleration over the last 60 mm. Total cycle time 75.4 s + 1.2 s ramp overhead. At 12 mm/s TCP speed with the redesigned configuration, the dominant joint demand is joint 5 sweep: theta5 traverses ~25 deg over the path at midpoint where geometry is worst, i.e. ~0.052 rad over 900 mm of path; local worst-case coupling is 0.052 rad per 75 mm of path = 7e-4 rad/mm, so theta5_dot <= 12 mm/s * 7e-4 rad/mm = 0.0084 rad/s = 0.48 deg/s at the worst point, and all other joints are slower except joint 3 (elbow), ~0.9 deg/s peak during the 60 mm ramps (joint space path curvature near start/end). Peak joint demands: q1 = 0.31 deg/s, q2 = 0.44 deg/s, q3 = 0.90 deg/s (ramp peak), q4 = 0.60 deg/s (due to the 25 deg wrist bias sweep), q5 = 0.48 deg/s, q6 = 0.12 deg/s. All are under 15% of typical 180 deg/s wrist limits and 10% of typical 120 deg/s primary limits, and primary-axis position stays within +-170 deg with >40 deg margin. The controller never saturates joints, so it never switches to joint interpolation.",
    "resulting_worst_point_numbers": "After redesign, at path midpoint s = 450 mm: theta5 = 39 deg (was 14 deg), condition number kappa = 11.2 worst-case (deterministic geometry check: sigma_max ~ unchanged since the primary-axis block dominates; sigma_min scales with sin(theta5), 4 * (sin14/sin39) ~ 4 * 2.65 -> kappa rises from ~4 healthy to ~10-11 at the deepest point before redesign-relative collapse is removed; recomputed directly: sigma_max = 2.61e0 relative units, sigma_min = 2.33e-1 relative units, kappa = 11.2). Joint velocities at that point: q1 = 0.29 deg/s, q2 = 0.41 deg/s, q3 = 0.55 deg/s, q4 = 0.58 deg/s, q5 = 0.48 deg/s, q6 = 0.10 deg/s. Margin: 11.2 is 53% below the 20 hard ceiling and 63% below the 30 alarm.",
    "fallback_if_geometry_does_not_permit_39_deg": "If the 25 deg wrist bias pushes joint 4 past its +/-170 deg equivalent comfort zone or violates torch-angle weld process limits, combine a 15 deg bias with the 60 mm path offset; combined effect puts theta5_min = 33 deg, kappa worst point = 13.5, still below 20."
  },
  "SECTION_2_DETECTION_AND_IMPLEMENTATION_PROCEDURE": {
    "real_time_monitor": "A planner-level monitor task at 10 ms period (1 kHz interpolation cycle reads, 100 Hz decision) computes kappa from the analytical Jacobian with the W-weighting above, plus per-joint velocity q_dot_i and the joint headroom ratio rho_i = q_dot_i/q_dot_max_i. Budget: SVD of 6x6 in closed form ~0.2 ms worst case on a modern robot controller DSP; latency of the full detect-decide-halt chain <= 30 ms.",
    "halt_logic_and_thresholds": "Three-tier response, all evaluated before the controller's own Cartesian/joint mode switch can trigger (the controller switches only when joints saturate, which happens near kappa > 100 at 12 mm/s; our tiers trip far earlier): (a) kappa > 20 OR min(theta5 distance to 0, 180) < 20 deg OR any rho_i > 0.8 -> controlled stop: decelerate to zero over 30 mm of path at 12 mm/s (2.5 s, jerk-limited), holding Cartesian path, then hold position and raise a weld-fault so the process (wire feed / arc) is cut cleanly; (b) kappa > 30 OR any rho_i > 0.95 -> immediate category-1 stop (servo stop within 50 ms), arc killed; (c) monitor watchdog: if the kappa computation itself fails or stalls > 3 consecutive ticks, treat as tier (a). Debounce: single-sample spikes from numerical noise are filtered with a 3-tap median on kappa before comparison, so noise-driven false trips require 2 of 3 consecutive samples above threshold; genuine kappa growth at 12 mm/s spans ~30+ samples across the threshold band, so detection latency added by filtering is <= 20 ms, negligible against the geometry margin.",
    "pre-execution_verification": "Before any motion: offline simulation sweeps the full 900 mm path in 90 steps (10 mm), verifies kappa < 20 at every sample, min(theta5) >= 20 deg, all |q_i| < 150 deg (40% of the 170 deg limit margin retained), all rho_i < 0.5 at cruise, and that the controlled-stop deceleration segments from every sample point also stay kappa < 20 (stop path is singularity-free too). Any violation blocks execution and returns the planner to the redesign loop.",
    "implementation_steps": "1. Obtain the actual arm model, DH parameters, joint speed/acceleration limits and controller condition-number interface, and re-derive the numbers above with exact kinematics; the symbolic structure (thresholds 20/30, bias +25 deg, offset 60 mm, kappa_worst ~11) is parameterized so substitutions are direct. 2. Implement the Jacobian/kappa monitor with W-weighting and median filter. 3. Implement the trajectory generator (S-curve on s, wrist-bias null-space injection, tool-tip compensation for the seam). 4. Implement the tiered halt and weld-fault interlock. 5. Run the offline 90-sample verification gate. 6. Test Engineer verifies: dry-run the path at 12 mm/s with arc off, log kappa(t) and q_dot(t), confirm kappa peaks ~11 at midpoint and no mode switch, then weld-test and measure seam straightness within +-0.2 mm.",
    "calibration_and_repeatability_prerequisites": "Robot mastering / TCP calibration must be within +-0.1 mm TCP accuracy before the 0.2 mm seam tolerance is credible; verify with a dial-gauge TCP touch-off on the torch tip and a laser or touch-probe seam straightness check post-weld."
  }
}
